      "value": "@{5a3c5fa04a8da67427803b3e|full_name} Absolutely! A very common use-case is custom post-processing. The entire Post-Processing 2 stack relies on fullscreen blits, performing different operations to modify the final image of the camera. This means that everything PostProcessing does, we could recreate, from the screen-space reflections to the custom color correction, and a host of other very interesting uses. I'm a big fan of screen-space color dithering, giving a cursed retro feel. PP2 actually has some API for implementing custom effects, although they're not exposed AFAIK.\n\nAnother common one is fully custom rendering, such as with raymarching SDFs. I have a prototype SDF renderer, making a metaball-based lava lamp run much faster than having to construct meshes each frame. Because I don't have access to blits, I'm currently using a vertex offset shader that just copies a quad's UV coordinates right to clip-space, and attaching that to a MeshRenderer. Unfortunately, there are a few headaches with this approach, like having to set it on its own layer to avoid affecting the reflection probes, having to turn it off when baking lighting, and having to modify its bounding box at runtime. It also has a small performance overhead compared to a blit, because it has to be handled by the CPU like it's an object in the world. That means layering, frustum and occlusion culling, and pray you remember to turn off its lightmaps and shadow casting. Very inelegant.\n\nVRChat has a very dedicated group of shader developers, and I've talked to many who would love access to these sorts of tools (Blit, read-only camera matrices & properties, etc), especially given how messy the workarounds are.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
