  "details": "Howdy! I'm really glad y'all added VRCGraphics, and it's understandable to want to curb world creators replacing the main camera's image, especially covering the menu with a custom shader, but I found a way.\n\nFirst, create a standard fullscreen quad shader.\n\nNext, write a new U# script with a reference to a texture and a material using said shader. \n\nFinally, using OnRenderObject(), call VRCGraphics.Blit(texture, material, -1, 0);\n\nAttach this script to an empty object in-world.\n\nIn my offline tests, I was able to completely cover the screen with a texture (including the menu). In RenderDoc, I saw the UI being rendered on a separate pass, but then my script drew the overlay shader again afterwards.\n\nNow, this is clearly an issue, since it'd be trivial to make a world that completely hides the ability to leave said world, thus becoming SAO. However, being able to blit to the main camera is a very, very, very useful feature for shader devs, and I would be very appreciative of the dev team if they could keep it in, only throwing out the call if the current camera is the UI camera. Please. I don't want to fill more worlds with gigantic quads with the same exact shader in them, it's awful.\n\nAttached is a screenshot of the issue, where you can see the UI is being blocked, and despite putting the alpha fairly low, it still made it very difficult to find the build number, let alone actually navigate out of the world.",
